# Architecture Rules

## Local-first

- Primary data (tasks, evidence, state) lives locally by default. Cloud sync,
  if ever added, is opt-in and secondary — never a requirement for the app to
  function.
- Prefer local storage (SQLite, local files, etc.) over remote databases for
  core app state.

## Connectors are isolated from UI

- Every external integration (Granola, Gmail/Gemini/Meet, Jira, Confluence,
  GitHub, Figma, Discord, local git) lives in its own connector module behind
  a shared interface (e.g. `fetchEvidence()`, `listItems()`).
- UI code must never import a connector's SDK/API client directly. UI talks
  only to a domain/service layer that aggregates connector output into the
  task model (evidence, next action, done criteria).
- Each connector is independently swappable/mockable/testable without
  touching UI code.

## Read-only by default

- All connectors default to read-only scopes/permissions when authenticating
  (e.g. Gmail readonly scope, GitHub read-only PAT, Jira/Confluence read
  access only).
- Do not request write scopes during initial integration setup unless a
  specific write feature is being built and explicitly approved.

## Central LLM router

- All LLM calls — regardless of feature or connector — go through one central
  LLM router module. No feature or connector may call a model provider's API
  directly.
- The router owns: provider/model selection, prompt construction conventions,
  retries, rate limiting, and logging of what was sent/received.
- New AI-powered features add a call site to the router, not a new client.

## Secrets

- Tokens and API keys are read and used only in backend/server-side or local
  main-process code. They must never be bundled into, sent to, or embedded in
  frontend/renderer code.
- Frontend code accesses connector data only through an authenticated local
  API/IPC boundary — never by holding credentials itself.
